{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low-bias in rising regimes: when the last 5–10 days show a clear uptrend, add an upward level shift to the whole path (model underestimates by ~+1 to +3 €/t, often larger around days 7–16).",
    "Turning-point lag: after a local peak/trough in the last ~3–7 days, the forecast tends to continue the prior direction too long; damp the first 1–2 weeks’ trend component and pull forecasts toward the recent 10–20d mean.",
    "Overshoot on late-horizon strength: the model often keeps prices elevated into weeks 3–4 when the realized path mean-reverts/downshifts; apply stronger mean reversion beyond ~day 15 (reduce levels by ~1–4 €/t when the model stays > recent mean).",
    "Underestimation of short-term volatility/spike amplitude: in the first ~10 days after an upswing, realized highs exceed forecast highs; widen bands and increase peak response (don’t cap rallies too early).",
    "Regime-change miss (sharp selloff): when recent history includes a sudden drop (large negative daily moves), the model is too high in the last 5–10 forecast days; apply a negative adjustment that grows with horizon (increasing downshift into days 20–30)."
  ]
}
